Objective

Exploit a UNION-based SQL Injection vulnerability to retrieve usernames and passwords from the users table and log in as the administrator.

Tools Used

Burp Suite Community Edition
Burp Repeater
PortSwigger Web Security Academy

Methodology

Intercepted the vulnerable request.
Sent the request to Burp Repeater.
Determined the number of columns using ORDER BY.
Confirmed that the query returned 2 columns.
Verified that both columns accepted string data using UNION SELECT with 'a'.
Retrieved usernames and passwords from the users table using a UNION query.
Logged in as the administrator using the retrieved credentials.

Skills Practiced

SQL Injection
UNION-based SQL Injection
Column Enumeration
String Data Type Identification
Data Extraction
Burp Suite Repeater

Key Learning

A successful UNION attack requires the same number of columns as the original query.
Compatible data types are required for each selected column.
Once string-compatible columns are identified, text-based data from other tables can be retrieved during authorized security testing.
